# =============================================================================================
# Dockerfile.gpu -- the GPU image for braingen_CondDiffuser_BraTS_v1 in CONDDIFF_MODE=live
# =============================================================================================
# Build:   docker build --platform linux/amd64 -f Dockerfile.gpu -t socr-image-gen-backend:gpu .
#          `--platform linux/amd64` for the same reason deploy-to-lightsail.sh passes it: the
#          only torch cu118 wheel is linux_x86_64, and a g4dn host is x86_64. Building on an
#          arm64 machine without it finds no wheel candidate and fails -- loudly, at least.
# Run:     see "THE RUN COMMAND" at the bottom of this header -- the --gpus flag is not optional
# Target:  EC2 g4dn.xlarge class (1 x Tesla T4, 16 GB VRAM, 4 vCPU, 16 GB RAM)
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY THIS IS A SEPARATE FILE AND NOT A BUILD ARG ON THE EXISTING Dockerfile
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The alternative considered was one Dockerfile parameterised by `ARG BASE_IMAGE` and
# `ARG REQUIREMENTS_FILE`. That is a real and often-correct pattern -- ARG is legal before FROM,
# so `FROM ${BASE_IMAGE}` does work. It was rejected for three reasons, in order of weight.
#
# 1. THE TWO BUILDS DIFFER IN THEIR STEPS, NOT JUST IN A VALUE. `python:3.10-slim` is a Debian
#    image whose entire purpose is to ship a Python interpreter. `nvidia/cuda:*-ubuntu22.04` is
#    a bare Ubuntu with the CUDA userspace bolted on and NO PYTHON AT ALL -- no `python`, no
#    `python3-pip`, no `venv` module. This file therefore needs an apt install and a virtualenv
#    (see below) that the CPU file must not have. A single file expressing that would need
#    shell conditionals inside RUN layers keyed off an ARG, which is exactly the kind of
#    Dockerfile nobody can read six months later, and which silently does the wrong half if the
#    ARG is misspelled at the command line (Docker does not error on an unknown --build-arg by
#    default; it warns).
#
# 2. IT KEEPS THE PRODUCTION BUILD PATH OUT OF THE BLAST RADIUS. `Dockerfile` builds the image
#    serving five GAN models that currently work. With a build ARG, every experiment on the GPU
#    branch is an edit to that file, and Docker parses the whole file on every build -- a
#    mistake in the GPU half can break the CPU build with no GPU involved. With two files, the
#    CPU path took exactly one line of change (its COPY, because of the requirements include)
#    and is then frozen. Deploy scripts call a bare `docker build ... .`, which resolves to
#    `Dockerfile` by Docker's default-filename rule, so they needed no edit at all.
#
# 3. ARG-BEFORE-FROM HAS A SCOPE TRAP. An ARG declared before the first FROM is OUTSIDE every
#    build stage; referencing it after FROM yields an EMPTY STRING unless it is re-declared
#    inside the stage. That failure is quiet -- you get `pip install -r ` or a blank tag -- and
#    it is a bad thing to have standing between this project and a production redeploy.
#
# THE HONEST COUNTER-ARGUMENT, since the same duplication argument was used to REJECT two
# requirements files (see requirements-common.txt): two Dockerfiles can drift too. The
# asymmetry is why the answer differs. A requirements file is a long list of version pins where
# drift is invisible -- a stale numpy on one target still installs, still imports, and only
# shows up as wrong numbers. A Dockerfile body is ~20 lines of structure where drift fails
# LOUDLY at build or boot: a missed COPY is "file not found", a wrong CMD is a container that
# exits immediately. So the pins are shared (one file, included by both) and the two short
# recipes are allowed to be two files. If a THIRD target ever appears, revisit this.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE CUDA PAIRING RULE -- READ BEFORE CHANGING EITHER SIDE
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#     requirements-gpu.txt   torch==2.0.1+cu118      <-->      this file   FROM nvidia/cuda:11.8.*
#
# These two lines are ONE decision written in two places. cu118 means "built against CUDA 11.8",
# and the base image must provide a CUDA 11.8 runtime. Change one, change the other, in the
# same commit.
#
# WHY A MISMATCH IS THE MOST DANGEROUS FAILURE THIS DEPLOYMENT HAS: IT IS SILENT.
# There is no exception, no traceback, no HTTP 500, and the images that come out are CORRECT.
# The chain is:
#       torch cannot initialise CUDA
#   ->  torch.cuda.is_available() returns False
#   ->  conddiff_inference.py:417 `DEVICE = "cuda" if torch.cuda.is_available() else "cpu"`
#       resolves to "cpu"
#   ->  every /generate request runs the 50-step DDIM loop on 4 vCPUs
#   ->  ~2 minutes a sample instead of ~1 second
#   ->  the request blocks uvicorn's event loop (api.py:81 is an `async def` wrapping
#       synchronous work), /health stops answering, and any health check with a kill threshold
#       recycles the container mid-request
# So the entire GPU migration is unachieved while the build, the push and the boot all report
# success. Nothing fails; it is just slow and occasionally restarts.
#
# THE FOUR WAYS TO LAND IN THAT STATE, all of which present identically:
#   (a) the CPU wheel got installed -- e.g. someone pointed this build at requirements.txt, or
#       added /whl/cpu as a second index. Diagnostic: `torch_version` ends in `+cpu`.
#   (b) base/wheel CUDA mismatch -- a cu118 wheel on a CUDA 12.x base, or vice versa.
#   (c) THE HOST DRIVER IS TOO OLD. A CUDA 11.8 runtime wants an R520+ driver (or R450+ relying
#       on CUDA minor-version compatibility). Nothing in this image can fix that; run
#       `nvidia-smi` ON THE HOST before building anything.
#   (d) THE CONTAINER WAS STARTED WITHOUT `--gpus all`. This is the easiest one to do by
#       accident, because the image is perfect and the command is short. Without it the NVIDIA
#       container runtime injects no device nodes and no libcuda.so.1, and torch sees nothing.
#
# HOW TO PROVE IT WORKED, and do this BEFORE timing anything with a stopwatch -- a stopwatch
# answers ambiguously and these answer exactly:
#       curl -s http://<host>:8000/conddiff-selftest | python -m json.tool
# Required rows (conddiff_inference.py:1538-1548 builds them):
#       "mode":           "live"
#       "device":         "cuda"          <- "cpu" here on a GPU host IS the failure above
#       "cuda_available": true
#       "torch_version":  "2.0.1+cu118"   <- a "+cpu" suffix can never see the card
#       "cuda_built":     "11.8"          <- must match this file's base image tag
#       "gpu_name":       "Tesla T4"      <- null means torch is not talking to the card
# Inside the container, `nvidia-smi` (present because of the CUDA base) separates cause (d) from
# the rest: if nvidia-smi cannot see the card either, the problem is the run command or the
# host driver, not the wheel.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# IMAGE SIZE -- WHAT THIS COSTS AND WHY IT IS ACCEPTED
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Order of magnitude, uncompressed, to be MEASURED with `docker images` rather than trusted:
#       CPU image today                       ~1.0-1.5 GB
#       CUDA 11.8 runtime base alone          ~2-3 GB
#       + torch cu118 and its nvidia-* deps   ~2-3 GB
#       => this image                         ~5-7 GB
# That is 4-5x the CPU image and it is simply the price of CUDA; there is no clever way around
# it while running real convolutions on a GPU. It is accepted because the constraint it trades
# against is gone: this image is pulled ONCE onto a persistent EC2 host with an EBS volume, not
# pushed on every deploy into a $10/month Lightsail container. Disk is cheap on g4dn; two
# minutes per sample is not.
#
# TWO SIZE LEVERS IF IT EVER MATTERS, both to measure before pulling:
#   - `-runtime` not `-devel`. Already done below. The devel tag adds nvcc and the full CUDA
#     toolkit, several GB more, and nothing here compiles CUDA.
#   - The cuDNN duplication. PyTorch's linux wheels generally vendor their own CUDA libraries
#     as `nvidia-*-cu11` pip packages, which would make the base image's copies redundant
#     weight. CHECK, do not assume, with
#         docker run --rm --entrypoint du <image> -sh /opt/venv/lib/python3.10/site-packages/nvidia
#     If the vendored set is complete, the plain `11.8.0-runtime-ubuntu22.04` base (no cudnn8)
#     saves ~1 GB. Do NOT take the further step of dropping to a non-CUDA base: `nvidia/cuda`
#     images also set NVIDIA_VISIBLE_DEVICES / NVIDIA_DRIVER_CAPABILITIES, which is part of how
#     the container runtime knows to expose the card, and they carry nvidia-smi, which is the
#     only in-container tool that distinguishes failure (d) from failures (a)-(c).
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE MODEL ASSETS: COPY AT BUILD, NOT MOUNT AT RUNTIME -- AND WHY
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Live mode needs, on top of the code:
#       inference/model/diffusion_ema.pt              ~341 MB   the fp32 EMA checkpoint
#       inference/conddiff_bundle/manifest.json                 the index
#       inference/conddiff_bundle/cond_slices/*.npy   ~42 MB    9-channel float16 conditioning
# None of it is in git, and it cannot be: GitHub hard-rejects any single file over 100 MB, and
# even an fp16 safetensors export is ~171 MB. The bundle arrives by sftp from Great Lakes.
#
# RECOMMENDATION: COPY, which is what the unchanged `COPY . .` below already does -- the
# operator stages the files into backend/inference/ before running `docker build`.
#
# Four reasons:
#   1. IT IS ALREADY THE ESTABLISHED MECHANISM, not a new burden. backend/inference/model/ is
#      empty in git today (`git ls-files backend/inference/model/` returns nothing) and the five
#      production GAN .pth files get there exactly this way: a human drops them in, `COPY . .`
#      bakes them into the image, and .dockerignore does not exclude inference/. Anyone who can
#      deploy the CPU container has already done this. Introducing a bind-mount only for the
#      diffusion assets would mean two different rules for weights sitting in the same folder.
#   2. THE IMAGE BECOMES THE UNIT OF PROVENANCE. This backend serves a published model. An image
#      digest that fully determines the weights means "which checkpoint produced this figure" is
#      answerable from the deployment record alone. With a mount, the answer lives in a host
#      path that nothing versions and a `docker run` line typed by hand months ago.
#   3. IT FAILS EARLY AND LOUDLY. A missing file is caught by _check_assets() at import, so
#      /conddiff-selftest reports ready:false immediately after boot. A mis-typed `-v` source
#      path does not error -- Docker CREATES an empty directory at the mount point and SHADOWS
#      whatever the image had there, so the container starts clean and every request refuses.
#   4. IT SURVIVES A MOVE OFF `docker run`. If this ever goes to ECS/Fargate or back to a
#      container service, the deployment API takes an image reference and there is no host
#      filesystem to bind-mount from. COPY works on every one of those; a mount works on none.
#
# THE COSTS, STATED PLAINLY: ~383 MB added to every push and pull, and changing a checkpoint
# means a rebuild. Both are tolerable because the checkpoint is FROZEN -- it produced the
# paper's numbers and is not iterated on.
#
# THE ESCAPE HATCH, for when you genuinely are iterating (a step sweep, a re-export):
#       docker run ... -v /srv/conddiff_bundle:/app/inference/conddiff_bundle:ro ...
# A mount shadows the baked-in copy at that path. Use it for experiments; do not make it the
# deployment. And re-read reason 3 first -- a typo in that source path is silent.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE RUN COMMAND
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   docker run -d --name socr-backend --restart unless-stopped \
#     --gpus all \
#     -p 8000:8000 \
#     -e SUPABASE_URL="..." -e SUPABASE_KEY="..." -e FRONTEND_URL="https://..." \
#     -e CONDDIFF_MODE=live -e CONDDIFF_STEPS=50 \
#     socr-image-gen-backend:gpu
#
# `--gpus all` requires the NVIDIA Container Toolkit installed on the EC2 host; the AWS Deep
# Learning AMIs ship it. CONDDIFF_MODE is passed explicitly even though this image already
# defaults it to live (see the ENV below): an operator reading the run command should be able
# to tell which mode production is in without reading Python. CONDDIFF_STEPS=50 is a
# PLACEHOLDER inherited from conddiff_inference.py, not a measured recommendation -- the paper
# renders at 200. Set it from the step sweep once that exists.
# =============================================================================================

# --- BASE: CUDA 11.8 runtime on Ubuntu 22.04 -------------------------------------------------
# THE TAG IS THREE PAIRED DECISIONS, none of them free:
#
#   11.8.0  <- PAIRED WITH requirements-gpu.txt's torch==2.0.1+cu118. See the pairing rule above.
#             This is the load-bearing half of the tag.
#   runtime <- NOT `-devel` (adds nvcc and the full toolkit, several GB, and nothing here
#             compiles CUDA) and NOT `-base` (omits the CUDA runtime libraries and cuDNN
#             entirely, which is the mismatch this file exists to prevent). `cudnn8` is included
#             because every convolution in the U-Net goes through cuDNN.
#   ubuntu22.04 <- CHOSEN FOR ITS PYTHON, which is the non-obvious part. Ubuntu 22.04's system
#             python3 is 3.10 -- the SAME MINOR VERSION as the CPU image's python:3.10-slim.
#             That matters because wheels are built per minor version (cp310), so both images
#             resolve requirements-common.txt to the same wheel files and the shared pin list
#             means what it says. ubuntu20.04 would give Python 3.8 and ubuntu24.04 Python 3.12,
#             either of which would quietly make the two targets different environments in a
#             second dimension while the requirements files looked identical.
FROM nvidia/cuda:11.8.0-cudnn8-runtime-ubuntu22.04

# Set working directory
# IDENTICAL TO THE CPU IMAGE ON PURPOSE, AND LOAD-BEARING. Every inference module builds its
# paths as os.path.join(os.getcwd(), "inference/...") -- conddiff_inference.py:331-336,
# cgan_inference_v2.py:122, wgan_inference.py:159, gan_3d_inference_v1.py:77. Combined with
# `COPY . .` (which copies the CONTENTS of backend/ into /app) and uvicorn being launched from
# here, os.getcwd() == "/app" and those resolve to /app/inference/... . Change this line and
# every model on the site stops finding its weights, with no other change anywhere.
WORKDIR /app

# --- Python, which the CUDA base does not have -----------------------------------------------
# This whole layer has no counterpart in the CPU Dockerfile: python:3.10-slim already IS a
# Python image. nvidia/cuda is a bare Ubuntu with the CUDA userspace added, so the interpreter,
# pip and the venv module all have to be installed. This is reason 1 in the "why a separate
# file" argument at the top -- it is a difference in STEPS, not in a value.
#
# gcc/g++ mirror the CPU image's system dependencies so that any pin needing a source build
# behaves the same on both targets.
#
# DEBIAN_FRONTEND=noninteractive is scoped to this RUN with a shell prefix rather than set as a
# container-wide ENV: some apt packages (tzdata) block on an interactive prompt at build time
# and would hang the build forever, but leaking that setting into the RUNTIME environment would
# also change how anything exec'd into this container later behaves. One-shot is enough.
#
# --no-install-recommends keeps apt from dragging in documentation and suggested packages;
# rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/* discards the ~40 MB package index in the SAME layer, because a
# separate `RUN rm` would leave it in the previous layer and shrink nothing.
RUN DEBIAN_FRONTEND=noninteractive apt-get update && \
    DEBIAN_FRONTEND=noninteractive apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends \
        python3.10 \
        python3.10-venv \
        python3-pip \
        gcc \
        g++ \
    && rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*

# --- A virtualenv, rather than installing into the system Python -----------------------------
# WHY BOTHER, when the CPU image installs straight into its system Python? Because "the system
# Python" means something different on the two bases. On python:3.10-slim it is an interpreter
# that exists to be pip'd into. On Ubuntu it is an OS component that apt owns, and pip writing
# into it is increasingly refused outright: PEP 668 marks distro interpreters
# EXTERNALLY-MANAGED, and pip then hard-errors with "error: externally-managed-environment"
# rather than installing. Ubuntu 22.04's pip 22.0.2 does not enforce it yet, so a system
# install would work TODAY and break the first time this base tag is bumped -- which is exactly
# the kind of latent failure to spend three lines avoiding now.
#
# Putting the venv first on PATH means every later `pip` and `python` in this file, in the
# HEALTHCHECK and in CMD resolves to the venv's without any activation step -- `activate` is a
# shell function and does not survive between Docker layers, so PATH is the correct mechanism.
#
# It also creates the `python` name. Ubuntu ships `python3` only, with no unsuffixed `python`
# binary; the venv's bin/ provides both, which is why the HEALTHCHECK and CMD below can be
# written the same way as the CPU image's.
ENV VIRTUAL_ENV=/opt/venv
RUN python3.10 -m venv $VIRTUAL_ENV
ENV PATH="$VIRTUAL_ENV/bin:$PATH"

# Copy requirements first for better caching
#
# BOTH FILES, AND THE SECOND ONE IS NOT OPTIONAL. requirements-gpu.txt's first directive is
# `-r requirements-common.txt`, the shared pin list. pip resolves that include relative to the
# directory of the file containing it (/app here) and reads it off disk at install time, so
# copying only requirements-gpu.txt fails with
#     ERROR: Could not open requirements file: [Errno 2] ... 'requirements-common.txt'
#
# requirements.txt (the CPU variant) is deliberately NOT copied here. Naming it would put it in
# this layer's cache key for no benefit, and -- much worse -- having it adjacent invites
# someone to "helpfully" add it to the pip line below. That would put the /whl/cpu index in the
# same pip run as /whl/cu118, because index options in a requirements file are GLOBAL to the
# invocation rather than scoped to the lines under them, and the resolver would be choosing
# torch from a candidate set containing both builds. It still reaches /app via `COPY . .`
# further down, after pip has finished, which is harmless.
COPY requirements-gpu.txt requirements-common.txt ./

# Install Python dependencies
#
# THE ONLY LINE IN THIS FILE THAT DIFFERS FROM THE CPU IMAGE BY A FILENAME. requirements-gpu.txt
# = requirements-common.txt (every shared pin) + torch==2.0.1+cu118 from the PyTorch cu118
# index. Point this at requirements.txt by mistake and you get a working, correct, CPU-only
# container on a GPU host -- failure (a) in the list at the top of this file, which no test
# except /conddiff-selftest will tell you about.
#
# --no-cache-dir matters far more here than on the CPU image: pip's wheel cache would otherwise
# retain a second copy of the ~2 GB torch download inside the layer.
RUN pip install --no-cache-dir --upgrade pip && \
    pip install --no-cache-dir -r requirements-gpu.txt

# Copy all backend files
#
# THIS IS ALSO HOW THE MODEL WEIGHTS GET IN -- see "THE MODEL ASSETS" at the top. .dockerignore
# excludes caches, .env and test files but NOT inference/, so whatever the operator has staged
# into backend/inference/model/ and backend/inference/conddiff_bundle/ is baked in here. That is
# the same mechanism the five GAN .pth files already use.
#
# NOTE THE SILENT MODE: if the assets were not staged, this COPY does not fail -- it simply
# copies fewer files. The build succeeds, the container boots, and _check_assets() reports
# ready:false on /conddiff-selftest. Check that endpoint after every rebuild; do not treat a
# green build as evidence the checkpoint is present.
COPY . .

# Create data directory for fallback storage
RUN mkdir -p /app/data

# Send Python's stdout/stderr straight to the Docker log instead of buffering it.
#
# WHY IT IS HERE AND NOT IN THE CPU IMAGE: Lightsail collects logs through its own agent, but
# this host is a plain `docker run`, so `docker logs` is the only view of what the process is
# saying. conddiff_inference.py prints its resolved mode, paths and asset status at import
# (line ~1325) -- exactly the diagnostics needed after a boot -- and with the default block
# buffering on a non-tty those sit unflushed in a 4-8 KB buffer, so a container that is starting
# up (or wedged) looks silent. This makes the startup banner appear when it happens.
ENV PYTHONUNBUFFERED=1

# The live-mode default, baked in.
#
# conddiff_inference.py:309 ALREADY defaults CONDDIFF_MODE to "live" when the variable is
# absent, so this line changes no behaviour. It is here to make the IMAGE self-describing:
# `docker inspect` on this image states its intent without anyone reading Python, and the
# gallery/live question cannot be decided by an omission in a hand-typed run command.
#
# The mirror image of this is the CPU deploy scripts, which pass CONDDIFF_MODE=gallery
# EXPLICITLY into the deployment JSON they generate. That is a safety interlock, not a
# preference: the Lightsail micro container (0.25 vCPU / 1 GB) cannot hold a 341 MB fp32
# checkpoint, and a live request there blocks uvicorn's event loop until the health check
# replaces the container -- taking the five working GAN models down with it. Two targets, two
# explicit values, and neither one relying on a default.
#
# `docker run -e CONDDIFF_MODE=gallery` still overrides this, which is the correct precedence:
# useful for serving the pre-rendered gallery from this host while the bundle is in transit.
ENV CONDDIFF_MODE=live

# Expose port
EXPOSE 8000

# Health check
#
# THIS DIFFERS FROM THE CPU IMAGE'S, DELIBERATELY, IN THREE WAYS -- because it is the only
# health check on this host. Lightsail ignores the Dockerfile HEALTHCHECK and uses the
# publicEndpoint healthCheck in its deployment JSON instead; a plain `docker run` has nothing
# else, so this one has to actually be right.
#
#   raise_for_status() -- the CPU image's check calls requests.get() and discards the result,
#   so a 500 from a backend that has failed to load its models still counts as healthy. The
#   only thing it can detect is a process that is not listening at all. One extra call makes it
#   mean what its name says.
#
#   timeout 30s (vs 10s) -- api.py:81 is an `async def` wrapping synchronous work, so a live
#   DDIM request occupies the event loop and /health cannot be answered until it finishes.
#   At ~1 s a sample on a T4 that is brief, but n_images loops sequentially and the first
#   request also pays a one-off ~341 MB checkpoint load. A tight timeout would classify a busy
#   container as a dead one. This is the same coupling that makes live mode impossible on
#   Lightsail; here the numbers are small enough that a generous timeout absorbs it.
#
#   start-period 90s (vs 40s) -- torch with CUDA takes appreciably longer to import than the
#   CPU wheel, and CUDA context initialisation on first use adds several seconds more.
#   Failures during the start period do not count against retries.
#
# `requests` is not a direct pin in requirements-common.txt; it arrives as a transitive of
# diffusers (and of supabase). That is worth knowing rather than discovering: the CPU image's
# identical line threw ImportError every 30 s for as long as nothing pulled requests in, and it
# went unnoticed only because Lightsail was not using it. If diffusers is ever dropped, this
# line breaks -- pin requests explicitly at that point rather than deleting the check.
HEALTHCHECK --interval=30s --timeout=30s --start-period=90s --retries=3 \
  CMD python -c "import requests; requests.get('http://localhost:8000/health').raise_for_status()"

# Run the application
#
# --workers 1 IS NOT A LEFTOVER FROM THE 0.25-vCPU CONTAINER; IT IS REQUIRED HERE FOR A
# DIFFERENT REASON. Each uvicorn worker is a separate PROCESS with its own address space and
# its own CUDA context, and conddiff_inference.py caches the U-Net in a module-level singleton
# (_MODEL). Two workers means two ~341 MB copies of the weights resident in VRAM plus two CUDA
# contexts (a few hundred MB each), and requests landing on different workers would contend for
# the single T4's SMs -- turning two concurrent 1-second samples into two slower ones rather
# than into parallelism. The T4's 16 GB would survive it; the point is that it buys nothing.
# Real concurrency for this model is the async job pattern described in CONDDIFF_MODEL.md 8.8,
# not more workers.
CMD ["uvicorn", "api:app", "--host", "0.0.0.0", "--port", "8000", "--workers", "1"]
